================================================================================
                    JIRA PRACTICALS LAB MANUAL (1 TO 12)
           Course: Artificial Intelligence Application Development & Jira
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 1-A: CREATE A JIRA CLOUD ACCOUNT AND EXPLORE THE DASHBOARD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Open Google Chrome and search for "Jira".
2. Click on the official Jira by Atlassian link ("Try Jira Free" / "Sign Up Free Today").
3. Choose "Continue with Google" and sign in using your Gmail account.
4. On the account setup screen, enter your site name / URL and click "Continue".
5. Answer the introductory questions:
   - What kind of work do you do? Select "Project Management".
   - Select a template: Choose "Kanban" and click "Continue".
6. Select the types of work you track and click "Next".
7. Click "Go to Jira" to launch the dashboard and explore the default interface.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 1-B: CREATE A COMPANY-MANAGED SCRUM/KANBAN PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Navigate to the top navigation bar and click on "Projects" (or "Spaces").
2. Click "Create Project" (or "Create Space").
3. Select "Kanban" (or "Scrum") from the project templates and click "Use Template".
4. Select "Company-managed" in the project type configuration option.
5. Provide the project name and key, then click "Next".
6. On the "Bring your team along" screen, add team member email addresses or select "I'll do this later", then click "Next" to land on the dashboard.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 1-C: CONFIGURE PROJECT DETAILS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Open the project created in Practical 1-B.
2. In the left-hand navigation sidebar, click on "Project settings" (or "Space settings").
3. Under the "Details" section:
   - Modify the Project Name, Project Key, and Project URL if needed.
   - Assign the Project Lead and Default Assignee.
   - Upload or select a custom project avatar/icon.
4. Click "Save details" / "Save changes".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 2-A: ADD TEAM MEMBERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Open your target Jira Project.
2. Click on "Project settings" -> "People" (or click "Add People" / "Share" on the top right).
3. In the pop-up dialogue box, enter the email address(es) of the user(s) to invite.
4. Assign an initial role from the dropdown menu (e.g., Member / Administrator).
5. Click "Add" / "Add person" to send the invitation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 2-B: ASSIGN ROLES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Go to "Project settings" -> "People".
2. Locate the added user in the member list.
3. Click the "Role" dropdown next to the user's name.
4. Select the appropriate role (e.g., Administrator, Developer, Viewer, Service Desk Team).
5. Confirm the assignment.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 3-A: CREATE AND CONFIGURE ISSUE TYPES (EPIC, STORY, TASK, BUG)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. In your project dashboard/backlog, click the "+ Create" button at the top (or under the Backlog column).
2. Click the "Issue type" dropdown menu.
3. Observe available standard issue types: Epic, Story, Task, and Bug.
4. Create sample items:
   - Select "Task", enter summary, and press Enter/Save.
   - Select "Bug", enter summary and description, and press Enter/Save.
5. To configure issue types, navigate to "Project settings" -> "Issue types" to view or modify custom fields and schemes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 3-B: CREATE COMPONENTS AND VERSIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Components (logical subdivisions or modules):
   - Go to "Project settings" -> "Components".
   - Click "Create component".
   - Enter Component Name (e.g., "UI", "Database", "Authentication"), Description, and Component Lead.
   - Click "Save".
2. Versions (releases/milestones):
   - Go to "Project settings" -> "Versions" (or "Releases" in the project menu).
   - Click "Create release" / "Create version".
   - Enter Version Name (e.g., "v1.0.0"), Start Date, Release Date, and Description.
   - Click "Save".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 4-A: CREATE ISSUES UNDER AN EPIC
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Click the "+ Create" button at the top header.
2. Set "Issue Type" to "Epic".
3. Enter the Epic Name / Summary (e.g., "ELMS - E-Learning Management System") and click "Create".
4. Open the created Epic from the backlog or board.
5. In the "Child issues" section of the Epic, click "+" to create linked child issues:
   - Add Stories, Tasks, or Bugs directly underneath.
   - Alternatively, create a standard issue and set the "Parent" / "Epic Link" field to this Epic.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 4-B: ASSIGN ISSUES TO USERS AND SET PRIORITY & DUE DATE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Open an existing issue (e.g., "Specification Understanding").
2. In the right-hand details pane:
   - Assignee: Click and pick a team member from the user list.
   - Priority: Click and select priority (Highest, High, Medium, Low, Lowest).
   - Due Date: Click the calendar picker and select a target completion date.
3. Close the panel (click 'X'); changes are updated automatically.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 5-A: DESIGN A CUSTOM WORKFLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Navigate to "Project settings" -> "Workflows".
2. Click "Add Workflow" -> "Add Existing Workflow" (or "Add Workflow" to design from scratch).
3. Select "Classic default workflow" (or a chosen template) and click "Next".
4. Associate the workflow with an issue type (e.g., Epic) and click "Finish".
5. Switch to the "Diagram" view to visualize the status nodes and transition pathways.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 5-B: ADD TRANSITIONS AND CONDITIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. While in the workflow canvas, click "Edit Workflow" to enter draft mode.
2. Click "Add Transition":
   - Select the "From status" (e.g., In Progress).
   - Select the "To status" (e.g., Done).
   - Enter a name for the transition (e.g., "Complete Work") and click "Create".
3. Click on the transition arrow, then click "Add Rule" (or Conditions / Validators).
4. Choose a condition/rule (e.g., "Only Assignee can execute this transition") and click "Select" / "Add".
5. Click "Update Workflow".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 5-C: PUBLISH AND ASSOCIATE WORKFLOW WITH A PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. In the draft workflow editor, click the "Publish" button.
2. When prompted to backup or associate, choose the workflow scheme for your project.
3. Review any status mapping prompts for existing issues.
4. Click "Acknowledge" / "Associate" to make the workflow active for your project.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 6-A: CREATE AND CONFIGURE SCRUM BOARD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Click on "Projects" -> "Create Project".
2. Select the "Scrum" template and click "Use template".
3. Select project type (Company-managed / Team-managed) and enter project details.
4. Click "Next" / "Create".
5. Once loaded, click the "..." menu on the top right of the board -> "Board settings" to configure columns, swimlanes, and estimation statistics (Story Points).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 6-B: CREATE AND START A SPRINT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Navigate to the "Backlog" from the left project menu.
2. Click "Create Sprint" at the top of the backlog list.
3. Add issues to the sprint by clicking "+ Create" inside the sprint container or dragging existing backlog items into it.
   Sample Sprint Backlog:
   - User Registration (Story, 3 Story Points)
   - User Login (Story, 3 Story Points)
   - Product Catalog (Story, 5 Story Points)
   - Shopping Cart (Story, 8 Story Points)
   - Checkout Process (Story, 13 Story Points)
4. Click "Start Sprint".
5. Enter the Sprint Name, Duration (e.g., 2 weeks), and Sprint Goal, then click "Start".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 6-C: COMPLETE SPRINT AND GENERATE SPRINT REPORT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. On the active Scrum board, drag completed tasks across to the "DONE" column.
2. Click the "Complete Sprint" button on the top right.
3. If any incomplete issues remain, choose whether to move them to the Backlog or to the Next Sprint.
4. Click "Complete".
5. Jira automatically navigates to the "Sprint Report", showing the burndown data, completed story points, and sprint summary.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 7-A: CREATE A COMPANY-MANAGED KANBAN PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Go to "Projects" -> "Create Project".
2. Choose "Kanban" and click "Use template".
3. Select "Company-managed".
4. Enter project name (e.g., "Online Shopping Site") and key, then click "Next".
5. Invite team members on the "Bring your team along" page (or skip) and click "Next".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 7-B: CONFIGURE KANBAN BOARD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Open the Kanban project board.
2. Create initial backlog cards: Click "+ Create", select issue type "Task", type the title, and hit Enter.
3. Open a task card to assign it to a team member, set the priority level, and specify a due date.
4. Click "..." on the top right -> "Board settings" -> "Columns":
   - Add columns (e.g., Backlog, Selected for Development, In Progress, Review, Done).
   - Set Maximum and Minimum WIP (Work In Progress) limits per column.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 7-C: TRACK ISSUE PROGRESS AND GENERATE CONTROL CHART
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Track Progress:
   - Drag and drop issue cards across columns (from "To Do" -> "In Progress" -> "Done") as development progresses.
2. Generate Control Chart:
   - Click "Reports" in the project menu.
   - Under Agile reports, select "Control Chart".
   - Analyze cycle time and lead time variations for issues resolved over the time period.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 8: JQL QUERIES AND SAVED FILTERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Part A - Write Basic JQL Queries:
   - Go to "Filters" in the top navigation bar -> "View all filters" (or "Search issues").
   - Click "Switch to JQL".
   - Execute queries:
     * Status Filter:   status = "In Progress"
     * Assignee Filter: assignee = currentUser()
     * Priority Filter: priority = "High"
     * Combined Query:  project = "ELMS" AND status = "In Progress" AND priority in ("High", "Highest")
2. Part B - Save Filters:
   - Click the "Save filter" button above the search results.
   - Enter a Filter Name (e.g., "My High Priority Tasks") and set sharing permissions.
   - Click "Save".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 9-A: GENERATE BURNDOWN CHART AND VELOCITY CHART
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Open your Scrum project and click "Reports" on the left menu.
2. Click "Burndown Chart":
   - Observe the guideline vs. actual burndown curve over the sprint duration.
3. Return to "Reports" and click "Velocity Chart":
   - Review committed vs. completed story points across past completed sprints.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 9-B: CREATE PIE CHART / ISSUE STATISTICS REPORT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Navigate to "Reports" in your project.
2. Scroll to "Issue Analysis" and click "Pie Chart Report".
3. Configure the report parameters:
   - Select Filter or Project.
   - Statistic Type: Select "Assignee", "Status", or "Priority".
   - Click "Next" to generate the interactive pie chart.
4. Optional: Under "Issue Analysis", select "Recently Created Issues Report" to review issue creation rates over specified time intervals.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 9-C: CREATE A CUSTOM DASHBOARD WITH GADGETS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Create Dashboard:
   - Click "Dashboards" on the top navigation bar -> "Create dashboard".
   - Enter Dashboard Name (e.g., "Project Health Dashboard") and set access rights.
   - Click "Save".
2. Add Gadgets:
   - Click "Add gadget" on the top right of your newly created dashboard.
   - Add desired gadgets from the gadget directory (e.g., "Pie Chart", "Assigned to Me", "Filter Results", "Sprint Burndown Gadget").
   - Configure each gadget's filter/project settings and click "Save".
   - Click "Done" to finalize the dashboard layout.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 10-A: VIEW AND MODIFY PROJECT PERMISSION SCHEME
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Go to "Project settings" -> "Permissions".
2. Review existing project permissions (e.g., Browse Projects, Create Issues, Edit Issues, Transition Issues).
3. Click "Actions" -> "Edit permissions" (requires Jira Administrator privileges).
4. Update permission grants for project roles, groups, or individual users, then click "Save".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 10-B: CONFIGURE PROJECT ROLES AND ASSIGN PERMISSIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Go to "Project settings" -> "People" (or Jira Administration -> System -> Project roles).
2. Check existing roles (Administrators, Developers, Service Desk Team, Viewers).
3. Click "Add people", type the member's email/username, and select the specific project role.
4. Click "Add" to assign permissions mapped under that role to the user.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 11-A & 11-B: CREATE AND CONFIGURE AN AUTOMATION RULE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Go to "Project settings" -> "Automation" (or the lightning bolt icon on the top right).
2. Click "Create rule".
3. Configure Trigger:
   - Select "Field value changed" (or "Issue transitioned").
   - Set "Fields to monitor for changes" to "Status".
   - Set "For" to "All work item operations". Click "Next".
4. Configure Condition:
   - Click "Add condition" -> "Issue fields condition".
   - Select Field: "Status", Condition: "equals", Value: "Done". Click "Save".
5. Configure Action:
   - Click "Add action" -> "Send email" (or "Assign issue").
   - Enter recipient (e.g., reporter or project lead), subject line, and email body.
   - Click "Next".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 11-C: TEST AND ENABLE AUTOMATION RULE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Provide a name for the rule (e.g., "Notify On Done Status").
2. Click "Turn on rule" / "Turn on flow".
3. To test:
   - Open an active issue on your board.
   - Transition the status of the issue to "Done".
   - Verify that the notification email is triggered and delivered to the recipient.
   - Check the "Audit log" under Project settings -> Automation to verify successful execution.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 12-A: INSTALL AND CONFIGURE THE JIRA MOBILE APP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Download App:
   - Android: Open Google Play Store.
   - iOS: Open Apple App Store.
   - Search for "Jira Cloud by Atlassian" and tap "Install".
2. Launch App:
   - Tap the Jira icon to open the app.
   - Select "Log In".
   - Sign in using your registered Google/Atlassian credentials.
3. Configure App:
   - Tap "Set Up Jira".
   - Set notification preferences (Allow notifications / Direct notifications).
   - Configure Do Not Disturb or critical alert permissions as prompted.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRACTICAL 12-B: VIEW AND UPDATE ISSUES AND SPRINTS FROM MOBILE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Select Project/Space:
   - Open the app, tap "Spaces" / "Projects", and select your project (e.g., "Online Shopping Site").
2. Create & Update Issues:
   - Tap the "+" icon to create a new Story/Task/Bug.
   - Open any issue card to update Status, add Comments, or reassign Assignees.
3. Mobile Sprint Management:
   - Tap the "Backlog" tab.
   - Tap "+ Create" to add items into the sprint.
   - Tap "Start Sprint", verify sprint duration/dates, and tap "Start Sprint".
   - Transition issue cards between columns directly on the mobile board.
4. View Summary & Metrics:
   - Tap the "Summary" tab on the project board to inspect completion rings, status overviews, and priority breakdowns in real-time.
